<h1>🌳 Growing Trees — English Worksheet</h1>
<p><strong>Topic:</strong> Two counters in one loop · <strong>Course:</strong> 3D Coordinates · <strong>Level:</strong> Extension · <strong>Time:</strong> about 40 minutes</p>
<p><span class="timing">Type <code>del</code> before every <code>run</code>. Every time.</span></p>
<p>Every <code>???</code> is one short thing for you to work out. There are no answers on this sheet.</p>
<hr />
<h2>1 · One tree, two fills 🌲</h2>
<p>A tree is a thin pole and a fat box.</p>
<pre><code class="language-python">blocks.fill(OAK_LOG,
    pos(x, 0, z),
    pos(x, trunk_h, z), REPLACE)
</code></pre>
<p><strong>In the trunk fill, which of x, y, z is different between the two corners?</strong></p>
<p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p>

<p><strong>So what shape do you get when only one of the three changes?</strong></p>
<p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p>

<pre><code class="language-python">blocks.fill(OAK_LEAVES,
    pos(x - leaf, trunk_h + 1, z - leaf),
    pos(x + leaf, trunk_h + 3, z + leaf), REPLACE)
</code></pre>
<p><strong>One corner uses minus. The other uses plus. Why?</strong></p>
<p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p>

<p><strong>If <code>leaf</code> is 2, how wide is the leaf box? Count the middle block too.</strong></p>
<p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p>

<hr />
<h2>2 · Two counters ✏️</h2>
<pre><code class="language-python">    trunk_h = trunk_h + 2
    leaf = leaf + 1
    x = x + 8
</code></pre>
<p>Start values: <code>x = 4</code>, <code>trunk_h = 4</code>, <code>leaf = 2</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fill the table.</strong></p>
<table class="tr">
<tr><th>i</th><th>x</th><th>trunk_h</th><th>leaf</th><th>leaf width</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>2</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="mini-q">Look down the columns.</div>

<p><strong><code>trunk_h</code> goes up by 2. <code>leaf</code> goes up by 1. Which tree is tallest, and which has the widest leaves?</strong></p>
<p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p>

<hr />
<h2>3 · Build it 🌳</h2>
<div class="tpl">Template</div>

<pre><code class="language-python">def on_chat():
    x = 4
    z = 8
    trunk_h = 4
    leaf = 2
    for i in range(1, ???):
        blocks.fill(OAK_LOG,
            pos(x, 0, z), pos(x, ???, z), REPLACE)
        blocks.fill(OAK_LEAVES,
            pos(x - ???, trunk_h + 1, z - ???),
            pos(x + ???, trunk_h + 3, z + ???), REPLACE)
        trunk_h = trunk_h + ???
        leaf = leaf + ???
        x = x + ???
player.on_chat(&quot;trees&quot;, on_chat)
</code></pre>
<div class="where"><span class="lab">Careful</span>
The three counter lines go at the <b>end</b> of the loop, after both fills. Put them first and your first tree comes out wrong.</div>

<p><strong>Before you run: how many trees will you get?</strong></p>
<p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p>

<div class="check"><span class="lab">Works when</span> three trees stand in a row, each taller and wider than the one before.</div>

<div class="stuck"><span class="lab">All the trees are the same?</span>
Your counter lines are outside the loop. Check the spaces at the front.</div>

<div class="stuck"><span class="lab">Only one tree?</span>
`x` is not changing, so every tree is built in the same place.</div>

<hr />
<h2>4 · The gap 📏</h2>
<p>Tree 3 has <code>leaf = 4</code>, so its leaves are 9 wide.</p>
<p><strong>Trees are 8 apart. Is 8 enough space for a 9 wide tree? Try it and look.</strong></p>
<p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p>

<p><strong>Change the 8 to a smaller number. Run it. What happens to the leaves?</strong></p>
<p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p>

<div class="check"><span class="lab">You understand it when</span> you can say the smallest gap that keeps every tree separate.</div>

<hr />
<h2>5 · Clean up 🧹</h2>
<p>Your trees reach x = 24 and y = 11.</p>
<div class="tpl">Template</div>

<pre><code class="language-python">blocks.fill(AIR,
    pos(0, 0, 0), pos(???, ???, ???), REPLACE)
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Your old <code>del</code> used 15. Why is that not big enough now?</strong></p>
<p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p><p class="ruled">&nbsp;</p>

<hr />
<h2>6 · Show Your Work 📸🎥</h2>
<p>Record <strong>one video</strong>, one take, no stopping. A phone is fine. Show these in order:</p>
<blockquote>
<ol>
<li>Type <code>del</code>, then <code>trees</code>. Walk past all three trees.</li>
<li>Show the trunk fill and say why it makes a pole.</li>
<li>Show the leaf fill and point at the minus and the plus.</li>
<li>Show your three counter lines and say what each one grows.</li>
<li>Show your filled table and read row 3 out loud.</li>
</ol>
</blockquote>
<p>Try to use these words: <strong>loop</strong>, <strong>counter</strong>, <strong>trunk</strong>, <strong>leaves</strong>, <strong>gap</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Plan what you will say before you record.</strong></p>
